<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=human-genome-project">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Human Genome Project</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1990">1990</a>–<a href="/years/2003">2003</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as HGP</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Human Genome Project was an international scientific research project with the goal of determining the base pairs that make up human DNA and of identifying, mapping and sequencing all of its genes. Planning began in 1984 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and the project was formally launched in 1990 by the US <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Energy and the National Institutes of Health. A rough draft was announced in June 2000, and the project was declared complete on 14 April 2003, covering about 92% of the genome. A parallel private effort by Celera Genomics was launched in 1998. A gapless sequence of the 22 autosomes and the X chromosome was published in January 2022.</p></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1990–2003</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>United States</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Craig Venter</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>A parallel privately funded effort by Celera Genomics, launched in 1998 by Craig Venter, raced the public project; the draft sequences were announced jointly in June 2000.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/dna-double-helix">Structure of DNA<span>1953</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=human-genome-project">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
